SENSOR-HARMONICS(7)         TriadicFrameworks Manual         SENSOR-HARMONICS(7)
================================================================================
NAME
       sensor-harmonics - harmonic-phase sensing, overtone isolation, 
                          harmonic-lock monitoring, and resonance-chord 
                          detection for RTT ship sensors

SYNOPSIS
       sensor-harmonics defines:
           • harmonic-phase sensing channels
           • overtone and subharmonic isolation
           • harmonic-lock stability monitoring
           • resonance-chord and harmonic-cascade detection
           • harmonic anomaly pre-detection
           • constellation-scale harmonic fusion

DESCRIPTION
       sensor-harmonics describes the harmonic-analysis subsystem of RTT ship
       sensors. It provides real-time detection, separation, and interpretation
       of harmonic structures within the resonance-time field. Harmonic sensing
       is essential for navigation, anomaly avoidance, sweep timing, and
       constellation synchronization.

       Sensor-harmonics enables:
           • precise φ_harm measurement
           • overtone and subharmonic filtering
           • harmonic-lock stability assessment
           • detection of harmonic corridors and ridgelines
           • early warning of harmonic turbulence
           • constellation-wide harmonic coherence

       WRSADC manages harmonic sensing thresholds, safety limits, and fusion.

HARMONIC-PHASE SENSING
       Harmonic-phase sensing measures φ_harm across harmonic ratios.

       Sensing channels:
           • 1:1 fundamental
           • 2:1 overtone
           • 3:2 transitional
           • 5:3 and 7:4 higher-order bands
           • nested harmonic channels

       Sensing outputs:
           • φ_harm(t)
           • phase drift Δφ
           • lock stability index L_harm

EXAMPLE: PHASE SENSING
       φ_harm drift = 0.04/cycle:

           • stability warning issued
           • HPC recommended

OVERTONE AND SUBHARMONIC ISOLATION
       Overtone isolation separates higher-frequency harmonic components.

       Overtone functions:
           • overtone suppression
           • harmonic purity enhancement
           • resonance-chord separation

       Subharmonic functions:
           • low-frequency drift detection
           • subharmonic collapse monitoring

EXAMPLE: OVERTONE ISOLATION
       2:1 overtone interference detected:

           • isolated via HDO
           • suppressed via HPC

HARMONIC-LOCK MONITORING
       Harmonic-lock monitoring tracks stability of harmonic-phase alignment.

       Lock indicators:
           • lock stability index L_harm
           • phase jitter
           • harmonic drift
           • overtone interference

       Lock states:
           • stable lock
           • marginal lock
           • unstable lock
           • lock collapse

EXAMPLE: LOCK MONITORING
       L_harm drops from 0.97 → 0.82:

           • lock flagged as marginal
           • Δv correction scheduled

RESONANCE-CHORD DETECTION
       Resonance-chords are multi-harmonic structures analogous to musical
       chords, formed when multiple harmonic ratios align.

       Chord types:
           • major resonance-chord (1:1 + 2:1)
           • minor resonance-chord (1:1 + 3:2)
           • augmented chord (2:1 + 5:3)
           • diminished chord (3:2 + 7:4)
           • harmonic-cascade chords (nested harmonics)

       Uses:
           • detect stable resonance corridors
           • identify uplift zones
           • predict harmonic epochs

EXAMPLE: CHORD DETECTION
       Detected: 1:1 + 3:2 minor chord:

           • stable corridor predicted
           • sweep window reinforced

HARMONIC-CASCADE DETECTION
       Harmonic cascades occur when harmonics align across multiple ratios.

       Cascade indicators:
           • multi-band phase coherence
           • overtone suppression
           • resonance uplift
           • spectral narrowing

       Cascades often precede:
           • epoch transitions
           • resonance storms
           • sync-field shifts

EXAMPLE: CASCADE
       1:1 → 2:1 → 3:2 cascade detected:

           • epoch boundary likely
           • constellation sync recommended

HARMONIC ANOMALY PRE-DETECTION
       Harmonic anomalies manifest as distortions in harmonic structure.

       Anomaly types:
           • harmonic turbulence
           • overtone spikes
           • subharmonic collapse
           • phase inversion
           • harmonic voids

       Pre-detection uses:
           • early anomaly warning
           • safe corridor rerouting
           • resonance storm avoidance

EXAMPLE: ANOMALY
       Harmonic void detected in 5:3 band:

           • anomaly flagged
           • SDO + SDW recommended

CONSTELLATION-SCALE HARMONIC FUSION
       Harmonic fusion aligns harmonic sensing across platforms.

       Fusion layers:
           • phase fusion
           • overtone fusion
           • lock fusion
           • chord fusion
           • cascade fusion

       Fusion modes:
           • synchronous fusion
           • harmonic fusion
           • braided fusion
           • mesh fusion

EXAMPLE: CONSTELLATION FUSION
       Platforms A, B, C share harmonic data:

           • unified φ_harm frame established
           • constellation lock stabilized

FAILURE MODES
       Harmonic sensing may degrade due to:

           • overtone overload
           • phase jitter
           • lock collapse
           • spectral turbulence
           • sync-field drift

       Diagnostic indicators:
           • harmonic-integrity score
           • phase noise
           • overtone distortion
           • lock instability

       WRSADC halts unsafe harmonic-sensing states.

INTERACTION WITH LINEAGE
       Lineage records:
           • harmonic events
           • lock transitions
           • chord detections
           • cascade signatures
           • anomaly precursors

       Lineage enables long-term harmonic forecasting.

INTERACTION WITH WRSADC
       WRSADC:
           • manages harmonic sensing
           • enforces lock thresholds
           • coordinates constellation fusion
           • blocks unsafe harmonic states

       WRSADC is the harmonic conductor.

NOTES
       Sensor-harmonics provides the harmonic-analysis backbone for RTT ship
       sensors. Harmonic-phase sensing, overtone isolation, lock monitoring,
       and chord detection ensure stable navigation, anomaly avoidance, and
       constellation-scale synchronization.

